Maniac wrote:
> [1] What to wish for if you get an early chance
>
> For the early first wish, would it be "good enough" to recommend
>  - blessed +X gray dragon scale mail
> for those without MR and
>  - blessed +X silver dragon scale mail
> for wizards and others that already have MR? That combination
> satisifies two (MR & armor) or three (MR & reflection & armor) of the
> "essentials" currently listed in the wishing section.

Yes, those or an MR-granting artifact are far and away the best
choices.

> After that, should we recommend a good weapon before or after
> reflection?

No, wishing for a weapon is a waste.  If you're recommending subsequent
wishes, go with jumping/speed boots, magic marker, or maybe an "oLS for
new players I guess, or artifacts with good powers (orb of fate, eye of
aethiopica, master key, etc) or even =conflict over a weapon.

You can sac for a weapon, or worst-case get crowned.  For lawfuls, #dip
for Excalibur. Heck, for twoweaponers using a silver saber and a
longsword (or whatever) is plenty of stopping power once you enchant
them up.

> Based on the discussion so far, there doesn't seem to be a consensus on
> +2 or +3. I also checked the wishing spoiler - it says +2 as well. Can
> someone make a compelling story to pick one over the other?

Wishing for +3 gets you an average of +1.8.  Wishing for +2 gets an
average of +1.6.  Unless you're going to be draining things back down
and trying to get them all to +5 (which is basically a vanity
operation), wishing at +3 makes more sense.

The exception is if you're doing amounts of scrolls/potions and having
2 is much more helpful than having 1, but having 3 isn't much more
helpful than 2.

> [3] NOT recommending gauntlets of dexterity nor of power
>
> I guess I am not sure why this is an unwise wish if you have the wishes
> to spare (and have already satisified the "essentials"). From a brief
> review of the code, boosting strength and dexterity appear to improve
> the effects in attacks (both physical and spell). Are these effects
> insignificant?

Gauntlets of power destroy spellcasting, so they only make sense for
non-casters.
Humans can get to 18/** which will give them the same benefits as GoP.
The incremental advantage of GoP is much less for strong characters
than weak ones.

So GoP only makes sense as a wish for poor casters who have low
str--but if you're early enough in the game to fit that description,
there are almost certainly better wishes.

Dex affects to-hit, but if your luck is maxed out (which it will be
soon after doing mines' end) and you're XL10 or so, you never miss
anything anyway.

Again, they are only a big deal early on when your wishes are much
better spent elsewhere.

Now, if you find early GoP that's a great thing, I'll usually wear them
until I get my strength up over 18 or get high enough level/good enough
equipment (robe) to have some hope at casting.
If you find GoDex, it's worth wearing them through the whole game, but
by the time you get to the castle you're better off just testing out
all the leather gloves there, finding one that's got a +2 or something
if you can, and saving the ?enchantArmor, marker charges, or wishes for
more valuable ends.

> Please also note that the MAG states in this section
>   Having said that, let's focus on the essentials first and then the
> nice to have items.
> both of these are in the "nice to have" items list (along with a lot of
> other items).

But they're not nearly as useful as speed/jumping boots, telecontrol,
magic markers, helm of brilliance, etc.

The GoP in particular are something that you'll usually wind up
ditching in favor of leather gloves before too long, not something you
burn a wish on.  If you're really strength-strapped, 2 or 3 blessed
tins of spinach or giant meat make more sense.

The GoDex are better since you'll wear them to the end of the game if
you happen to find them, but their value over leather gloves is really
minimal and I'd much rather have the wish for emergencies if it's from
a wand (and hence savable).  If it's not, a magic marker is far more
useful (especially for new players who are likely to forget their ?gold
detection, or blank them accidentally, or want to geno ; on water, or
whatever)

If I had infinite wishes, yeah, I'd get GoDex, but it'd be after
getting telecontrol, free action, conflict, and levitation rings,
unholy water, a can of grease, and other marginally useful items.

> The wishing spoiler also puts this into the "high priority" list and
> I've already moved it down one level in the MAG.

The wishing spoiler makes some dubious recommendations.  E.g. it flags
GoP as "especially for a Valk".  The only reason I can think of for a
Valk is if you're going to be throwing Mjollnir, which is certainly not
something we should be encouraging in a guide trying to get you safely
through to ascension.

It doesn't mention jumping boots (whoever wrote the spoilers seems to
have omitted them as a viable option everywhere, even though by eyeball
it looks like half the ascensions by experienced NAO players use
them).

I think _everything_ except weapons in any section of the wishing
spoiler (high, medium, low, special needs, and without wishing) is a
better wish choice than GoP with the exception of the luckstone.

> [4] should we recommend dip into fountains (or not) to get a wish
>
> It appears the consensus is that we should recommend the use of
> fountains (after you are buff enough), but I am still not sure we
> should be recommending hazardous ways to get wishes in MAG.

It's important to make sure that you really emphasize the dangers of
#dipping, and that it is only to be done when the alternative (not
dipping) is more dangerous.

> The MAG already mentions that a wish from a fountain will occur at
> 1/150th (or less) for each dip / quaff. I thought someone had done the
> math on the chance of getting a wish on average of dipping in the first
> 20 levels (including with sinks), but can't seem to find it. That would
> be a good piece of data. Also, if I read the code properly, you'll on
> average get about 15 bad outcomes (nymph, demon, or snakes - ignoring
> the preventable bad outcomes) for each wish.
>
> Is it really worth the risk for someone trying to get that first
> ascension?

Yes, because when you're XL10 AC -10 HP 120 with the +4 excalibur and
know how to run and write Elbereth, getting those 15 bad outcomes is
essentially zero risk.
And "descending to levels where destroy armor/touch of death/etc start
happening, polytraps rip your armor and cloak up, etc" is reasonably
high risk.

I certainly would recommend any even reasonably safe method of
acquiring MR before diving in the dungeon.

I try to get it before ever going below DL7, which often means
lingering above there until fountains are worth the gamble.
(polytraps can get generated starting at DL8, and usually gold nagas
start appearing somewhere around then)

If I have to go below DL7 without MR, I'm bee-lining for the Castle to
minimize the window of risk--as soon as I find a down staircase I take
it w/o exploring the levels DL8 and below fully.  I can come back when
it's safer to explore at a leisurely pace.

One of the key things a strategy guide should be doing is hammering
home that acquiring decent HP, decent AC, poison/sleep resistance, MC,
and MR/reflection are the early goals.
Fountain dipping (only once it's quite safe) fits with that philosophy.



